<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danube Swabians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Donauschwaben</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> Swabians are German-speaking people descended from settlers who moved into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a> and the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> reconquest from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the 18th century. Many came from south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, though not only from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> most fled or were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and many were interned or deported.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danube-swabians.jpg" alt="Danube Swabians" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Argean · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Danube_Swabians.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ethnic Germans first moved into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> in the 12th century, when German merchants and miners settled at the invitation of the Hungarian monarchy, but outside the colonies of the Carpathian Germans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a> settlement was not extensive. The Ottoman-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> wars of the 17th and 18th centuries devastated and depopulated much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> valley, and the Habsburgs resettled it with settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, the Palatinate and other regions. Their neighbours called them all Swabians because the first settlers were mostly Swabians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first wave of invited settlement followed the Ottoman defeat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683, encouraged by nobles and by officers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> in 1718, they planned its resettlement, along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a>, to restore farming.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> took the throne in 1740 she encouraged vigorous colonisation of crown lands and permitted the settlers to keep their language and religion, generally Roman Catholic; more than 100,000 Germans came between 1740 and 1790. The farmers drained marshes, rebuilt farms and built roads and canals.</p><p>In the 1930s Nazi Germany promoted its ideology among the Danube Swabians, claiming a right to protect them as Volksdeutsche. After the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1941, many served in German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> forces, including the 7th SS Volunteer Mountain Division Prinz Eugen, at first as volunteers and later under conscription. In November 1944 the AVNOJ Presidium declared the German minority in Yugoslavia collectively hostile, and its property was confiscated.</p><p>About 30,000 Danube Swabians, mostly women, were deported to forced labour in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donbas">Donbas</a>, where an estimated 16 per cent died. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> the old and young were interned in camps in Gakovo, Rudolfsgnad and elsewhere, and of about 170,000 interned from 1944 to 1948 some 50,000 died; the historian Arnold Suppan regards the destruction of the Danube Swabians as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Most survivors resettled in German-speaking countries, about 660,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and about 150,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, with others going to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Their speech, Schwowisch, mixes Swabian, East Franconian, Austro-Bavarian, Pfälzisch, Alsatian and Alemannic with Hungarian, Serbian and Romanian words, and their cooking, which uses paprika heavily, earned them the nickname Paprikadeutsche. Diaspora societies and clubs keep the language and customs, though their number is shrinking as the generations who lived in the homelands die.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Danube Swabians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tens of thousands of Catholic German settlers, later called <b>Danube Swabians</b>, were brought in alongside Serbs, Romanians and Italians, while Hungarian nobles were largely excluded.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-temeswar">Banat of Temeswar</a></li></ul></section>
